"Record view. A laser beam is split in two: the reference beam (a plane wave at angle θ_r) goes straight to the plate, and the object beam lights the object, whose scattered light also reaches the plate. Where the two overlap they interfere, and the plate records the intensity I(x) = |R + O|² along its surface (OpenStax University Physics Volume {#}, section {#}). Everything shown at the plate is computed exactly: the reference is a plane wave, a plane object wave comes from a mirror, and each object point sends out the exact cylindrical wave of the two dimensional slice (the Hankel function H₀(kr)). The beam ratio is the reference intensity over the object intensity at the plate's center. For two plane waves the fringes on the plate are λ/|sin θ_r − sin θ_o| apart, which is λ/({#} sin(θ/{#})) when the plate bisects the angle θ between the beams. The microscope shows the developed plate with its transmittance (light where little light fell); the curve above it is I(x). The cursors read to {#} μm, and the count is of the bright fringes (maxima of I) from cursor A to cursor B, counting one within {#} μm outside a cursor (a cursor set by eye on a bright fringe). The view of the beams is not to scale except for the angles and the positions of the object points; its wavefronts are drawn {#} times farther apart than they are.",
"Reconstruct view. The developed plate, with the object gone, is lit by a plane wave of wavelength λ₂ along the reference direction or from the other side of the normal. Just past the plate the light is u(x) = t(x) times the illuminating wave. It is split into plane waves with one FFT (the angular spectrum) and each plane wave is carried to any distance z exactly; behind the plate (z < {#}) the same plane waves are traced back, which is what a camera focused there would see. The map shows the result between z = −{#} and +{#} mm, on a log scale (each color step is a factor of {#}, three steps shown). Waves too steep to leave the plate (evanescent) are dropped; so is light that would leave the {#} mm computing window before reaching z, rather than letting it wrap round, and none of it reaches the part shown. The detector takes the light leaving within ±{#}° of its angle, as a fraction of the light falling on the plate (counting the flow through the plate). Predicted image positions come from tracing the rays that leave the plate's center and their neighbors (exact ray optics, the tangential focus); predicted beam angles from the grating equation sin θ = sin θ_i ± (λ₂/λ₁)(sin θ_o − sin θ_r).",
"What is simplified: the demo is a two dimensional slice, so each \"point\" is a bright line perpendicular to the slice and the plate is a strip {#} mm wide; a real hologram is two dimensional and also stores the third direction. The plate is thin: it records only the light at its surface, so it acts as a grating that works at any wavelength (a thick plate would also select the wavelength and angle, as white light holograms do). The emulsion is linear: the amplitude transmittance is proportional to the light that fell on it, t = I/I_max (a positive transparency); a real emulsion is linear only over part of its range. The plate does not change the phase of the light, and the beams are perfectly coherent and steady during the exposure.",
